  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a forward-and-inverse relationship between the neutron-star equation of state and the observable mass–radius relation. On the left, a family of smooth blue curves shows pressure versus energy density, representing possible theoretical equations of state. In the center, a neural-network-like model maps between these quantities and the observables. A dashed arrow across the top indicates the “well-posed problem”: starting from an assumed equation of state, one can predict a corresponding mass–radius curve. On the right, that prediction is shown as a red M–R track, with pink ellipses indicating scattered observational data or uncertainty regions around measured mass–radius values.\n\nA second dashed arrow along the bottom points in the opposite direction and is labeled as an “ill-posed inverse problem.” This emphasizes that inferring the underlying equation of state from noisy and incomplete mass–radius data is much harder, because many possible internal physics models may be compatible with the observations. Overall, the figure conveys that the forward physics mapping is straightforward, while the reverse reconstruction requires statistical or machine-learning methods to handle ambiguity and uncertainty.",
